WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.667 --> 00:00:02.335
The year is 1953.

00:00:02.335 --> 00:00:05.422
The US purchases this 
from Bell Labs...

00:00:05.797 --> 00:00:08.049
the first commercial 
silicon solar cell.

00:00:08.925 --> 00:00:12.429
But these first solar cells 
are not exactly efficient.

00:00:13.138 --> 00:00:14.472
If you were to put them 
on your roof

00:00:14.472 --> 00:00:16.182
and try to generate electricity

00:00:16.182 --> 00:00:19.060
it would cost you about 
$300,000 a month.

00:00:19.853 --> 00:00:23.565
Fast forward 70 years and 
that cost has come down.

00:00:23.565 --> 00:00:25.316
Way down.

00:00:26.026 --> 00:00:28.695
Solar energy is the cheapest 
it's ever been...

00:00:28.820 --> 00:00:32.115
nearly 90% cheaper than it was 
just in 2009.

00:00:32.365 --> 00:00:33.616
In a lot of places

00:00:33.616 --> 00:00:36.494
it is now cheaper to generate 
electricity with solar...

00:00:36.661 --> 00:00:39.539
than with coal, natural gas, 
or nuclear power.

00:00:40.457 --> 00:00:42.709
This is an unqualified 
success story.

00:00:43.084 --> 00:00:45.295
And if we can take the 
right lessons from it...

00:00:45.587 --> 00:00:47.964
this story can help us 
create and deploy

00:00:47.964 --> 00:00:50.467
all the other technology 
we will need

00:00:50.467 --> 00:00:52.635
to keep our planet livable.

00:00:56.056 --> 00:00:58.058
They just became cheap 
so quickly.

00:00:58.058 --> 00:00:59.851
It's really an amazing breakthrough

00:00:59.851 --> 00:01:02.562
that is going to come to define 
the 21st century.

00:01:02.771 --> 00:01:05.982
So how did solar energy go from 
prohibitively expensive

00:01:05.982 --> 00:01:07.859
to cheaper than fossil fuels?

00:01:08.985 --> 00:01:11.196
“We're in an energy crisis now.”

00:01:11.196 --> 00:01:13.656
In the 1970s, with 
the oil crisis

00:01:13.656 --> 00:01:14.949
there was a really big push

00:01:14.949 --> 00:01:17.494
on research and development 
for alternative energy.

00:01:17.869 --> 00:01:20.538
“Today, in directly harnessing 
the power of the sun

00:01:20.538 --> 00:01:22.916
we're taking the energy that God gave us...

00:01:23.249 --> 00:01:26.920
and using it to replace our dwindling 
supplies of fossil fuels.”

00:01:27.462 --> 00:01:29.506
With Project Independece, 
as it was called...

00:01:29.631 --> 00:01:32.675
the US federal government 
allotted more than $8 billion

00:01:32.675 --> 00:01:34.469
to solar research and development.

00:01:34.594 --> 00:01:36.012
That really made a huge difference

00:01:36.012 --> 00:01:38.306
and probably doubled 
the efficiency of the cells.

00:01:38.848 --> 00:01:42.852
It was a guy named Paul Maycock
who came from Texas Instruments.

00:01:42.936 --> 00:01:44.771
—Oh, like the calculators?
—Yeah!

00:01:44.771 --> 00:01:48.024
Working on calculators, 
and he found for calculators...

00:01:48.399 --> 00:01:51.152
the more they built, the cheaper 
the calculators got.

00:01:51.152 --> 00:01:53.530
And he said, same thing 
for solar.

00:01:53.530 --> 00:01:56.282
If we build more, the cost 
will come down.

00:01:56.324 --> 00:01:59.577
But before the US could really test
 Maycock’s idea

00:01:59.577 --> 00:02:02.413
President Reagan canceled 
Project Independence.

00:02:02.705 --> 00:02:05.375
Fortunately, two countries
were ready to step in

00:02:05.375 --> 00:02:07.919
and put Maycock’s hypothesis 
to the test.

00:02:09.671 --> 00:02:11.840
Like the US, Japan 
had been hit hard

00:02:11.840 --> 00:02:13.466
by the oil crisis in the 70s.

00:02:13.842 --> 00:02:16.094
They started funding solar 
research and development

00:02:16.094 --> 00:02:17.387
around the same time

00:02:17.387 --> 00:02:20.890
and soon they developed solar cells 
small and powerful enough

00:02:20.890 --> 00:02:23.143
to run watches, calculators, 
and toys.

00:02:23.393 --> 00:02:25.812
Trivial, in terms of the 
global energy system...

00:02:26.146 --> 00:02:29.983
but crucial in that large companies
were now becoming interested

00:02:29.983 --> 00:02:33.778
in solar and crucially, figuring out 
how to construct solar...

00:02:34.070 --> 00:02:35.905
at really low, low cost.

00:02:35.905 --> 00:02:38.408
That knowhow came in handy when 
the Japanese government

00:02:38.408 --> 00:02:41.786
announced a major subsidy for 
rooftop solar in the mid-90s.

00:02:42.120 --> 00:02:44.455
But the global market for 
solar technology

00:02:44.455 --> 00:02:45.790
was still relatively small.

00:02:46.332 --> 00:02:49.419
Until Germany stepped in and 
truly changed the game.

00:02:49.878 --> 00:02:51.129
The most important policy

00:02:51.129 --> 00:02:52.755
on this whole chain, 
if I had to pick one

00:02:52.755 --> 00:02:54.966
it would be the German feed-in tariff.

00:02:54.966 --> 00:02:56.176
Feed-in tarriffs.

00:02:56.176 --> 00:02:59.929
Which was basically a program 
that deployed solar at scale.

00:03:00.096 --> 00:03:01.389
Here's how it worked.

00:03:01.389 --> 00:03:03.349
Starting in 2000, the 
German government

00:03:03.349 --> 00:03:05.018
basically said to power companies...

00:03:05.018 --> 00:03:07.687
Hey, if you can start producing
renewable energy

00:03:07.687 --> 00:03:08.938
we will buy it from you.

00:03:08.938 --> 00:03:11.357
We promise for double the market price

00:03:11.357 --> 00:03:15.111
and we will keep paying you at that rate
for the next 20 years.

00:03:15.320 --> 00:03:20.950
And so for developers of solar projects
in Germany, all of a sudden...

00:03:21.367 --> 00:03:23.912
it became like a no-brainer 
to do this.

00:03:23.953 --> 00:03:26.623
Even though Germany is not 
the sunniest place in the world.

00:03:26.706 --> 00:03:29.167
Companies responded to 
the incentives by building

00:03:29.167 --> 00:03:30.668
solar farms like this one.

00:03:30.668 --> 00:03:33.338
In one year, the installations for solar

00:03:33.338 --> 00:03:35.798
went up by a factor of 4 
and then it just grew.

00:03:36.132 --> 00:03:39.010
The more panels they produced, 
the cheaper it got

00:03:39.010 --> 00:03:40.428
to generate electricity with solar.

00:03:40.929 --> 00:03:43.389
Then another country 
picked up the baton...

00:03:43.556 --> 00:03:47.310
and made solar cheaper than even 
Maycock could have imagined.

00:03:48.186 --> 00:03:49.604
So it really starts with

00:03:49.604 --> 00:03:51.731
Deng Xiaoping in the 1980s

00:03:51.731 --> 00:03:54.692
and had this plan for a thousand 
Chinese students to go abroad

00:03:54.692 --> 00:03:57.070
and come back and see what 
they came back with.

00:03:57.278 --> 00:04:00.448
One group of students went to 
the University of New South Wales

00:04:00.448 --> 00:04:02.408
in Australia, where they 
worked with a team

00:04:02.408 --> 00:04:04.244
that was producing some of 
the most efficient

00:04:04.244 --> 00:04:05.995
solar cells in the world.

00:04:05.995 --> 00:04:08.915
After a few years, one of those students 
went back to China...

00:04:09.165 --> 00:04:12.293
and opened up the country's first 
commercial solar manufacturer

00:04:12.293 --> 00:04:13.962
Suntech in 2002.

00:04:14.254 --> 00:04:16.089
With that new German law in place

00:04:16.089 --> 00:04:19.259
the company had so much success
selling solar panels to Germany...

00:04:19.634 --> 00:04:21.886
Suntech success attracted competitors...

00:04:22.053 --> 00:04:23.638
which helped drive down prices

00:04:23.638 --> 00:04:27.100
in part because all of this demand 
inspired Chinese manufacturers

00:04:27.100 --> 00:04:29.477
to produce at an unprecedented scale.

00:04:29.978 --> 00:04:31.312
Policymakers around the world

00:04:31.312 --> 00:04:34.816
noticed the falling prices and wrote laws 
to create new markets

00:04:34.816 --> 00:04:37.735
in their countries, including 
the United States.

00:04:37.986 --> 00:04:39.946
The United States passed 
an important law.

00:04:39.946 --> 00:04:42.615
It's called the Energy Policy Act 2005.

00:04:43.032 --> 00:04:45.034
And buried deep within that law

00:04:45.034 --> 00:04:47.078
was an investment tax credit.

00:04:47.620 --> 00:04:49.622
Sounds kind of boring, 
but it turns out

00:04:49.622 --> 00:04:51.874
it was absolutely critical 
to deploying solar...

00:04:52.125 --> 00:04:53.543
because what it said was that

00:04:53.543 --> 00:04:55.753
if you put out a new solar project...

00:04:55.962 --> 00:04:59.799
you can get 30% of those costs 
back for a tax credit.

00:05:00.091 --> 00:05:02.343
This sparked a phenomenon 
called solar leasing...

00:05:02.552 --> 00:05:04.887
where companies would look 
for places to put solar

00:05:04.887 --> 00:05:06.556
so that they could reduce the amount

00:05:06.556 --> 00:05:08.558
they owed the federal government in taxes.

00:05:09.267 --> 00:05:11.894
Other policies helped create 
markets for solar modules

00:05:11.894 --> 00:05:15.064
in Spain, Italy and crucially, 
in China.

00:05:15.356 --> 00:05:18.276
The Chinese devised their own 
subsidy program

00:05:18.276 --> 00:05:19.652
that was modeled on the German one

00:05:19.652 --> 00:05:21.571
where you get a guaranteed price.

00:05:21.571 --> 00:05:24.657
By 2011, China was not only 
the biggest producer of solar...

00:05:24.949 --> 00:05:27.577
but the biggest producer of solar electricity.

00:05:29.746 --> 00:05:31.372
In the course of a single lifetime

00:05:31.372 --> 00:05:34.500
solar energy has transformed 
from a niche technology

00:05:34.500 --> 00:05:37.295
to the cheapest way to bring 
clean, reliable power

00:05:37.295 --> 00:05:39.547
to billions of people around the world.

00:05:39.922 --> 00:05:42.550
But the markets that brought us 
these lower prices

00:05:42.550 --> 00:05:45.636
didn't just magically appear 
by some invisible hand.

00:05:45.887 --> 00:05:48.348
Political leaders in countries
all over the world

00:05:48.348 --> 00:05:51.059
created these markets, 
then subsidized them

00:05:51.059 --> 00:05:54.103
for decades to the tune 
of billions of dollars.

00:05:54.103 --> 00:05:56.356
By investing that money

00:05:56.356 --> 00:05:58.566
you got the solar to 
come down in costs

00:05:58.691 --> 00:06:00.943
to the point where you don't need 
to subsidize it anymore.

00:06:01.069 --> 00:06:02.612
We're doing that on purpose

00:06:02.612 --> 00:06:04.655
with things like batteries 
and electric vehicles.

00:06:05.031 --> 00:06:07.033
We're doing that with things 
like heat pumps.

00:06:07.033 --> 00:06:08.659
Solar provided us with 
a playbook 

00:06:08.659 --> 00:06:10.745
for how to do that for
other technologies.

